{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>You shall not do so to YHWH your Elohim, etc.</b> Since the nations choose for their error the high mountains and the flourishing tree, for the idols do not honor the place but the place honors them, and those who go after the error seek after the place more than after the error — and since the place is a great cause for that worship, therefore it is said: ״אַבֵּד תְּאַבְּדוּן אֶת כָּל הַמְּקוֹמוֹת״ and so on — but you, ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּן כֵּן לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״ — that you shall not be the ones choosing the place, ״כִּי אִם אֶל הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳״ and so on, for He, blessed be He, is the one who chooses and not you, for His honor, blessed be He, does not depend on the place, but the Holy One, blessed be He, honors His place. Therefore, ״לְשִׁכְנוֹ תִדְרְשׁוּ״ — you shall not seek after the place, but you shall seek His Presence: where is the place of His honor? For He, blessed be He, is the essence and the place is subordinate to Him. And regarding what is said, ״אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳״ — that He did not reveal the place immediately, the Moreh ha-Nevukhim gave three reasons for this, and Rabbi Isaac of Corbeil recorded them in his book." "And I say that there is a secret in the matter, for even to Abraham He did not reveal it immediately, and He said, ״עַל אַחַד הֶהָרִים אֲשֶׁר אֹמַר אֵלֶיךָ״ (Genesis 22:2), and he called its name ״ה׳ יִרְאֶה״ (ibid. 22:14), for He, blessed be He, sees, and there is none with us who knows how far. And it is reasonable to hear that this is why He did not reveal it — so that they would not treat Shiloh and Nov and Giv'on with contempt when they know with certainty that this is not the inheritance and the resting place. And you should know that the commentators said that here Shiloh and the Eternal House are alluded to, and they proved this by means of several changes they observed in the passage: for at first He said regarding Shiloh, ״כִּי אִם אֶל הַמָּקוֹם״ and so on, ״לָשׂוּם שְׁמוֹ שָׁם״ — the meaning is an incidental placement for the time being, and that is Shiloh. And afterward He said: ״וְהָיָה הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳ בּוֹ לְשַׁכֵּן שְׁמוֹ שָׁם״ — ״לְשַׁכֵּן״ implies permanence for all generations. In the first He said ״וְנִדְרֵיכֶם״, and regarding the Eternal House ״וּמִבְחַר נִדְרֵיכֶם״; and there He added ״וּשְׂמַחְתֶּם לִפְנֵי ה׳״, whereas in the first He did not mention ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״. In the first He said ״אַתֶּם וּבָתֵּיכֶם״, and regarding the Eternal House He added ״אַתֶּם וּבְנֵיכֶם וּבְנוֹתֵיכֶם״. In the second He said ״אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם בּוֹ״ — implying in the place, whereas in the first He did not mention ״בּוֹ״." "And on this I have a difficulty: behold, in the first it is said ״מִכָּל שִׁבְטֵיכֶם״, implying that the silver was collected from all the tribes, and Rashi proved this at the end of the book of Samuel (II Samuel 24:24) from what is written there: ״שְׁקָלִים חֲמִשִּׁים״, while in Chronicles (I 21:25) it is written: ״מִשְׁקַל שֵׁשׁ מֵאוֹת״ — rather, fifty shekels were collected from each and every tribe and it came to six hundred. If so, then certainly the first passage speaks of the Eternal House which David built. And furthermore I have a difficulty: was not the place of the Eternal House chosen from the days of eternity, and proof is from Adam, who offered there, and the event of the binding of Isaac proves this — so what is the meaning of what He said, ״אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳״, in the future tense? And what they said, that the expression שִׂימָה implies an incidental, temporary placing — my mind is not at ease with this explanation, for behold it is said (Deuteronomy 31:19): ״וְלַמְּדָהּ אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל שִׂימָהּ בְּפִיהֶם״ — and did HaShem command to put the Torah in their mouths in an incidental, temporary manner? And see what Rashi explained on the verse ״אֲשֶׁר תָּשִׂים לִפְנֵיהֶם״ (Exodus 21:1) — it implies, on the contrary, that the expression שִׂימָה indicates something fixed and established which shall never depart." "Therefore I say the reverse of this, and thereby everything will be resolved: for what is said, ״כִּי אִם אֶל הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳ מִכָּל שִׁבְטֵיכֶם לָשׂוּם שְׁמוֹ שָׁם״, speaks of the Eternal House, which was collected from all the tribes — and even though that place was already chosen from the days of the patriarchs, it nevertheless says ״אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר״ in the future tense, because its ultimate choice would take place through the collection of the silver that would come from all your tribes. Therefore it does not say ״יִבְחַר בּוֹ מִכָּל שִׁבְטֵיכֶם״, for ״בּוֹ״ refers to the place, and the place was already chosen; rather, what He said, ״אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר״, means that the collection would be chosen from all your tribes. ״לָשׂוּם שְׁמוֹ שָׁם״ — a placing of permanence. ״לְשִׁכְנוֹ תִדְרְשׁוּ״ — that is to say: seek from elders and the discerning, and they will tell you that this place was chosen for holiness from days of eternity. ״וּבָאתָ שָׁמָּה״ is in the singular, for this verse is an admonition to the many equivalent to an admonition to the individual — that they may not offer on a bamah; not only the many, where there is concern that their desire is to separate themselves from the congregation of Israel, but even an individual in whom there is no such concern — in any case he may not offer on a bamah. ״וְהֲבֵאתֶם שָׁמָּה״ and so on — that He did not say ״מִבְחַר נִדְרֵיכֶם״, is to teach that one should bring of the choicest, since this is an a fortiori inference from Shiloh, and furthermore, that even an incidental bringing brings a person success in this holy place with even greater elevation. And He said, ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם שָׁם לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — that even physical eating is לִפְנֵי ה׳ there, and a fortiori rejoicing, which is in the soul. And He said, ״אַתֶּם וּבָתֵּיכֶם״ — for presumably in Jerusalem they acquired for themselves perfect ones and likewise many important houses (cf. Nahum 1:12), and thereby He included all the members of his household." "And afterward He said, ״וַעֲבַרְתֶּם אֶת הַיַּרְדֵּן״ and so on, ״וְהָיָה הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם בּוֹ״ and so on — implying that this speaks of a place chosen close to the crossing of the Jordan, that is, Shiloh; and regarding it He said, ״אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר בּוֹ״, because He would ultimately choose in that place to lodge His name there for the time being — for the Mishkan is called by this name because it was temporary. ״וּשְׂמַחְתֶּם שָׁם לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — specifically the rejoicing that is in the soul is לִפְנֵי ה׳, and the whole matter is clear on its own. And that He returned and said, ״כִּי אִם בַּמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳ בְּאַחַד שְׁבָטֶיךָ״ — this too is Shiloh, which was in one of the tribes and not in all of them; and the verse comes to establish a negative prohibition in the matter, according to Rashi's explanation, even in Shiloh, and a fortiori in the Eternal House. And had He not returned and said, ״כִּי אִם בַּמָּקוֹם״ and so on, I would have said that what He said, ״הִשָּׁמֶר לְךָ״ — to establish a negative prohibition in the matter — that applies only to the Eternal House. And this explanation is true and correct."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :register {:flags {1 ["the Eternal"], 2 ["the Eternal"], 3 ["the Eternal"], 4 ["the Eternal"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:40.960033249Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}